\f0\fs24 \cf0 Pothole/Speed Breaker Detection Using YOLOv11\
===================================\
\
This project implements a real-time road hazard detection system using the YOLOv11 object detection model. It is designed to detect potholes and speed bumps in images and videos, helping enhance the safety and navigation of autonomous vehicles.\
\
-----------------------------------\
Project Files\
-----------------------------------\
- dataset.zip            	: Zipped YOLO-format dataset\
- test.mp4            	: Input test video\
- output.mp4            	: Output video with annotations\
- best.pt                	: Trained model weights\
- YOLOv11-Training/      : Folder generated after training with model checkpoints\
\
-----------------------------------\
Requirements\
-----------------------------------\
- Python 3.8+\
- Ultralytics YOLOv11\
- OpenCV\
- Matplotlib\
- Google Colab (recommended for GPU support)\
\
Install the required library:\
pip install ultralytics\
\
-----------------------------------\
Step 1: Dataset Setup\
-----------------------------------\
Place your dataset zip file in the working directory and unzip it by running line 1 of code:\
\
!unzip /content/dataset.zip\
\
Make sure the dataset contains:\
- data.yaml\
- train/images/\
- train/labels/\
- valid/images/\
- valid/labels/\
\
-----------------------------------\
Step 2: Model Training\
-----------------------------------\
from ultralytics import YOLO\
\
model = YOLO("yolo11l.pt")  # Load YOLOv11 base model\
\
model.train(\
    data="/content/dataset/data.yaml",\
    epochs=100,\
    imgsz=640,\
    batch=16,\
    project="YOLOv11-Training",\
    name="pothole-speedbump-detect"\
)\
\
-----------------------------------\
Step 3: Model Evaluation\
-----------------------------------\
metrics = model.val()\
print(metrics)\
\
-----------------------------------\
Step 4: Image Inference\
-----------------------------------\
Here make sure to have correct model path \'91best.pt\'92 and correct image path\
\
from ultralytics import YOLO\
import cv2\
from matplotlib import pyplot as plt\
\
model = YOLO("/content/YOLOv11-Training/pothole-speedbump-detect/weights/best.pt")\
\
img = cv2.imread("/content/dataset/train/images/your_image.jpg")\
results = model.predict(source=img, conf=0.4, stream=True)\
\
for r in results:\
    annotated = r.plot()\
    plt.imshow(cv2.cvtColor(annotated, cv2.COLOR_BGR2RGB))\
    plt.axis('off')\
    plt.show()\
\
-----------------------------------\
Step 5: Video Inference\
-----------------------------------\
Here make sure to have correct path to model \'91best.pt\'92 and uploaded test video with correct name. \
\
import cv2\
from ultralytics import YOLO\
\
model = YOLO("/content/best.pt")\
\
cap = cv2.VideoCapture("bump2.mp4")\
\
frame_width = int(cap.get(cv2.CAP_PROP_FRAME_WIDTH))\
frame_height = int(cap.get(cv2.CAP_PROP_FRAME_HEIGHT))\
fps = cap.get(cv2.CAP_PROP_FPS) or 30\
\
out = cv2.VideoWriter('output.mp4',\
                      cv2.VideoWriter_fourcc(*'mp4v'),\
                      fps,\
                      (frame_width, frame_height))\
\
while True:\
    ret, frame = cap.read()\
    if not ret:\
        break\
\
    results = model.predict(source=frame, imgsz=640, conf=0.4, stream=True)\
    for r in results:\
        annotated_frame = r.plot()\
        out.write(annotated_frame)\
\
cap.release()\
out.release()\
\
-----------------------------------\
Notes\
-----------------------------------\
- Adjust the confidence threshold as needed (default: 0.4)\
- Ensure you are using the correct path to your trained model (best.pt)\
- Run everything in a GPU-supported environment like Google Colab for best performance\
- The final output video will be saved as output.mp4\
\
